  "slug": "injury-cord-c274",
  "summary": "Recent preprints apply single-cell, spatial and perturbation methods to glia and microglia in spinal cord injury, aging and pelvic pain.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is glial and immune cell state in the spinal cord and related sensory tissue. Two rat studies examine injury directly: a single-nucleus and spatial atlas of photoactivated cAMP-driven repair, and a comparison of contusion and bilateral dorsal hemisection models that tracks neuroinflammation dynamics. A spatially resolved multi-omic atlas of mouse and human spinal cord associates aging and senescence markers with specific cell types. Microglial regulation is addressed in an isogenic stem cell model, where NGLY1 deficiency raises RUNX1 and alters microglial development, phagocytosis and cytokine expression, and in an arrayed Perturb-seq platform for dissecting microglial state dynamics. A chronic pelvic pain study profiles satellite glia, reporting sex-specific translatomes with mitochondrial and vesicular gene changes. The shared advance is the use of cell-type-resolved, spatial and perturbational approaches across injury, aging and glial contexts.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-22"
}
